"Contextuality of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes (https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02553)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Title: Contextuality of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes ... We present several findings, including the proof of a conjecture by Kim and Abramsky [1].\nTitle: Contextuality of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes\nAuthors: Derek Khu, Andrew Tanggara, Kishor Bharti\nDate: Tue Feb  4 18:23:24 2025\n\nQuantum error correction is vital for fault-tolerant quantum computation, with deep connections to entanglement, magic, and uncertainty relations. Entanglement, for instance, has driven key advances like surface codes and has deepened our understanding of quantum gravity through holographic quantum codes. While these connections are well-explored, the role of contextuality, a fundamental non-classical feature of quantum theory, remains unexplored. Notably, Bell nonlocality is a special case of contextuality, and prior works have established contextuality as a key resource for quantum computational advantage. In this work, we establish the first direct link between contextuality and quantum error-correcting codes. Using a sheaf-theoretic framework, we define contextuality for such codes and prove key results on its manifestation. Specifically, we prove the equivalence of contextuality definitions from Abramsky--Brandenburger's sheaf-theoretic framework and Kirby--Love's tree-based approach for the partial closure of Pauli measurement sets. We present several findings, including the proof of a conjecture by Kim and Abramsky [1]. We further show that subsystem stabilizer codes with two or more gauge qubits are strongly contextual, while others are noncontextual. Our findings reveal a direct connection between contextuality and quantum error correction, offering new insights into the non-classical resources enabling fault-tolerant quantum computation.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContextuality of Quantum Error-Correcting Codes | PRX Quantum (https://journals.aps.org/prxquantum/abstract/10.1103/7zb5-vs4x)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; To build a reliable quantum device, we need robust “spell-checkers,” known as quantum error-correcting codes. Our research connects this practical challenge with one of quantum mechanics’ strangest features: contextuality, where a measurement’s outcome depends on the other measurements performed alongside it. ... A crucial next step is to investigate whether more contextuality can lead to even better error correction, propelling the field toward more reliable and scalable quantum technologies.\n## Popular Summary\n\nQuantum computers promise breakthroughs in solving complex problems, but their fundamental building blocks, called qubits, are incredibly fragile. To build a reliable quantum device, we need robust “spell-checkers,” known as quantum error-correcting codes. Our research connects this practical challenge with one of quantum mechanics’ strangest features: contextuality, where a measurement’s outcome depends on the other measurements performed alongside it. While known to be a source of quantum advantage, its role in error correction was a mystery. We establish the first direct link, proving that contextuality is a mandatory ingredient for achieving universal quantum computation through certain key protocols.\n\nWe developed a new framework to classify error-correcting codes as “contextual” or “noncontextual,” identifying a simple structural rule that governs the distinction. We then applied this rule to “code-switching,” an important strategy for assembling the universal toolkit of operations required for any quantum algorithm. Our analysis reveals that the very structure that enables these protocols to achieve universal computation is precisely what makes them strongly contextual. This discovery bridges the gap between abstract quantum principles and the design of fault-tolerant hardware.\n\nThis connection provides a powerful new “litmus test” for quantum engineers: if a proposed error-correcting scheme is not contextual, it cannot be used to achieve universality through these powerful methods. Our findings pave the way for new design principles that leverage every available quantum resource. A crucial next step is to investigate whether more contextuality can lead to even better error correction, propelling the field toward more reliable and scalable quantum technologies.\n\n## References (111)\n\n  1. P. W. Shor, Scheme for reducing decoherence in quantum computer memory, Phys. Rev. A 52, R2493 (1995).\n  2. D. Gottesman, Stabilizer codes and quantum error correction, Ph.D. thesis, California Institute of Technology, 1997.\n  3. E. Dennis, A. Kitaev, A. Landahl, and J. Preskill, Topological quantum memory, J. Math. Phys. 43, 4452 (2002).\n  4. A. Y. Kitaev, Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons, Ann. Phys. 303, 2 (2003).\n  5. Quantum Error Correction, edited by P. A. Lidar and T. A. Brun (Cambridge University, Cambridge, United Kingdom, 2013).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCCO\nCommun. Comb. Optim.\nCommunications in Combina (https://oro.open.ac.uk/98050/9/98050final.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Conjecture 6.\nEvery graph has a terminal set.\nAs u is\nWr-equidistant, Wr ∪{u} is a clique; in particular, as W1 is a maximum clique of G,\nwe must have r > 1.\nIf follows that u must have been deleted in the reﬁning process during the r-th stage of\nthe algorithm. Hence there must be an s < r such that d(w, w′) = 2 and d(w, u) = 3\nfor all w ∈Ws, w′ ∈Wr; however, this implies that u is S-terminal, as it is the initial\nvertex of a shortest path of length three to Ws that passes through Wr. It follows\nthat S is a terminal set.\nIt has also been veriﬁed computationally that all graphs with order at most eleven\nhave termina