s, has remained largely unexplored.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGilbert–Pollak Conjecture | ProofAtlas (https://www.proofatlas.ai/collaboration/gilbert-pollak-conjecture/)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Image: Three ivory terminal points at the corners of an equilateral triangle connect by gold arms to one smaller central Steiner junction on a dark green field.An equilateral triple attains the proposed √3/2 ratio; this equality example does not settle the universal conjecture.On this pageProblem ... Statement boundary Research question, not proof evidence ... The equilateral triple attains S(V)/M(V)=√3/2; the open conjecture asks whether every finite planar terminal set satisfies this lower bound. ... Orientation-specific shallow-block compression remains live in O_Q, and a reflected graph-legal mechanism remains live in O_R. ... A 2026 preprint reports a certificate-backed improvement of the universal lower bound to 0.8559; that source-reported value remains below sqrt(3)/2 and is not a resolution. ... A 2025 paper proposes a different open bounded-terminal conjecture: among configurations of d+1 terminals, the regular d-simplex minimizes the ratio.\n\nSourced mathematical context\n\n## The known mathematical landscape\n\nContext collected Aug 27, 2026\n\nCurrent status Open conjecture\n\nThe universal Euclidean-plane Gilbert-Pollak conjecture remains open. In the convention rho = inf L_SMT/L_MST over finite planar terminal sets, it asserts rho = sqrt(3)/2. Du and Hwang published a claimed proof in 1990–1992, but later peer-reviewed analyses identified gaps in essential continuity and characteristic-area steps, so those papers do not establish the conjecture. A 2026 preprint reports a certificate-backed improvement of the universal lower bound to 0.8559; that source-reported value remains below sqrt(3)/2 and is not a resolution. The false unrestricted regular-simplex generalization in d