 at the Agency for Science, Technology and Research in Singapore and his colleagues provide an answer [4]. ... Within this partially closed setting, the researchers unified several previously distinct approaches to contextuality, resolving a standing conjecture and providing a unified framework for analyzing quantum error-correcting codes. ... In this way, contextuality is elevated from a property of special quantum states to an unavoidable structural feature of known universal fault-tolerant architectures based on code switching.\n\nThe researchers focused on error checks based on so-called Pauli observables and considered the stabilizer framework for subsystem codes, a class of quantum error-correcting codes in which some degrees of freedom are treated as auxiliary and need not be actively protected. Crucially, the team also enlarged the measurement scenario in a physically natural way. When two Pauli observables “commute,” the outcome of their product can be inferred from the outcomes of the individual measurements; accordingly, the effective measurement scenario should include these inferred observables as well. This construction leads to the concept of a so-called partial closure, which captures the full informational content accessible from a code’s error-checking data.\n\nWithin this partially closed setting, the researchers unified several previously distinct approaches to contextuality, resolving a standing conjecture and providing a unified framework for analyzing quantum error-correcting codes. From this framework emerges a strikingly sharp criterion: For subsystem stabilizer codes, strong contextuality arises if and only if the code contains at least two qubits that do not encode logical information. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnsolved problems in combinatorial games (https://library.slmath.org/books/Book71/files/GONC6-04.pdf)\nciteturn5search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Thea van Ro